/**
 * Pest library — recluse spiders in Texas, Loxosceles spp.
 *
 * THE HARDEST PAGE ON THE SITE TO WRITE HONESTLY, because the honest version
 * argues against the fear that sells the treatment.
 *
 * TWO TEXAS-SPECIFIC POINTS THAT MOST SITES GET WRONG:
 *
 * 1. THE SPECIES. texasinsects.tamu.edu records FOUR other Loxosceles in Texas
 *    besides L. reclusa: the Big Bend recluse L. blanda (WEST TEXAS), the Texas
 *    recluse L. devia (Central/South), L. apachea, and L. rufescens. It states
 *    "The Texas recluse may be the most common species in Texas and is often
 *    mistaken for L. reclusa." The TAMU spider checklist lists five Loxosceles
 *    for Texas.
 *    ⚠ SOURCE TENSION: Penn State lists Texas among states where L. reclusa is
 *    "established" and Vetter's range description reaches "into Texas". The
 *    honest resolution used here: L. reclusa reaches Texas but its core range
 *    is east and north; across most of WEST Texas the native recluses are
 *    L. blanda and L. apachea. So "brown recluse" as a blanket West Texas term
 *    is imprecise. This page says that.
 *
 * 2. THE OVER-DIAGNOSIS PROBLEM IS DIFFERENT HERE. In Florida and California
 *    the spider is essentially absent, so a diagnosis is almost always wrong.
 *    In Texas the spider is genuinely present — so the page must NOT simply
 *    import the "there are no recluses here" argument. The correct Texas
 *    framing: the spider is real, and a suspected bite is STILL far more
 *    likely to be something else.
 *
 * VERIFIED MEDICAL FIGURES:
 *  - Penn State Extension: ~90% of bites produce no reaction or a small (~5mm)
 *    red papule; ~10% result in dermonecrotic lesions; severe systemic effects
 *    in "less than 1 percent of bites".
 *  - Cacy & Mold (OKPRN, J Fam Pract 1999), 149 possible bites / 112 followed:
 *    43% healed within two weeks; necrosis in 40% AVERAGING 0.48 cm diameter;
 *    13% scarred; ONE hospitalisation; 27% systemic symptoms; NO deaths.
 *    ⚠ SELECTED SAMPLE — these were people who sought care, and only 15% had a
 *    spider identified; 54% merely "reported seeing a brown recluse in the
 *    area". Do NOT average this against the Penn State figure. Present both
 *    with their populations named, exactly as the page does.
 *  - DSHS: "There is no effective antivenom available."
 *  - JABFM 2004: systemic DAPSONE "associated with slower healing" and "an
 *    increased probability of scarring"; systemic CORTICOSTEROIDS "associated
 *    with slower healing". Both are commonly prescribed. Report it.
 *
 * OVER-REPORTING EVIDENCE (Vetter et al., Toxicon 2003; J Med Entomol 2004;
 * J Med Entomol 2005):
 *  - 216 physician diagnoses across CA/OR/WA/CO vs 35 verified specimens ever
 *    recorded from those four states combined.
 *  - Colorado spider survey: 26,000 spiders identified, TWO were Loxosceles.
 *  - Los Angeles survey: 2,500 spiders submitted, NONE were Loxosceles.
 *  - South Carolina: 478 physician reports in 1990 vs ONE authentic specimen.
 *  - Florida: 124 diagnoses across 31 counties 1997–2002 vs 11 verified
 *    specimens in 100 years of record.
 *  - National: 1,773 arachnids from 49 states submitted as suspected recluses;
 *    only 324 were L. reclusa, all but four from endemic areas; 23 of 26
 *    non-endemic states yielded zero.
 *  - THE KANSAS HOUSE: 2,055 brown recluses collected in six months, 400+ of
 *    bite-capable size, with NO apparent loxoscelism in the occupants. This is
 *    the single most powerful fact available on this species.
 *
 * ⚠️ DO NOT WRITE: any MRSA percentage. NOT VERIFIED. The page names MRSA as a
 *   known impostor without attaching a figure.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import Cta from '../../components/Cta.astro';

const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Pest library', path: '/pest-library/' },
  { name: 'Brown recluse', path: '/pest-library/brown-recluse/' },
];

const quickAnswer =
  'Recluse spiders are genuinely present in Texas — several native species, and in West Texas usually not the true brown recluse at all. They are also the most over-diagnosed bite in American medicine. Around 90 percent of actual recluse bites produce nothing worse than a small red bump, and the reliable identification feature is six eyes in three pairs, not the violin.';

const faqs = [
